{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for exact4a661bc0a7e297433704360ad5fbf397bf7899c7 and all seventeen artifacts, under review8df94cbd/promise479584fd and governing requestadd29762. The earlier mutex/I/O, cancellation, cold-observer and document-provenance corrections are recognized, as are this head's late-reply guard, within-wait request bound and rendered TopBar coverage. The following remaining defects prevent approval.\n\n1. Preserve the retirement barrier across every clock lifetime (internal/service/headwatch.go:86 and :105). If A is cancelled while its read is still returning, B waits on A. Cancelling B makes run return through ctx.Done and close B.done without waiting for A. C then waits only on the already-closed B.done and starts a read while A is still active. The controlled three-lifetime probe fails in all three runs; its read deliberately remains in its teardown window after cancellation. The two-lifetime positive control passes. Keep the barrier tied to all outstanding predecessor reads, even when intermediate clocks are cancelled before starting. Cancellation is not proof that a Git read has returned. Add the actual multi-retirement regression and retain prompt waiter/slot release.\n\n2. Bound pending rebuild work across wait lifetimes (ui/src/lib/rebuild.ts:36 and the stop function; ui/src/lib/store.ts:74). The new stopped guard correctly drops an obsolete reply and inFlight prevents overlapping ticks within one probe. But stop only clears the timer: it does not cancel the pending fetch, and the next wait creates a fresh inFlight flag. The real useWorkroom hook, driven through a controlled endpoint, accumulated three unresolved /v0/rebuild fetches across three completed waits. The test supports AbortSignal cancellation and counts requests still unresolved or un-aborted. Preserve one shared outstanding-request bound across waits, or cancel and release each request on wait completion/teardown before a new probe starts. The earlier late-reply and within-wait probes now pass; retain those controls and add this cross-wait case. Timer unref is not request cancellation or a concurrency bound.\n\n3. Keep condition2 open until its revised scope is resolved. Assessment11101b88 correctly distinguishes the browser and current-source cases, but its case-B claim is not established while finding2 remains, and case C is only unadopted proposal0b8a9ff5. The existing /v0/status implementation and CLI/MCP current-frontier checks do not by themselves prohibit a separately qualified observational surface, as requester's dispositionc4d5dce8 already explains. Obtain the requester's durable disposition on the revised case matrix, and ordinary adoption before any required new governed interpretation. Do not count filing the proposal as delivering case C or let a notifier/partial landing close the full request. No new endpoint implementation is authorized by this review.\n\nArchitecture: layers3,5 and7 are affected. Kernel verification, the cancellation-independent verified-snapshot flight and atomic publication remain unchanged; the clock is advisory notification and the browser qualifier is client state. The named clock and UI lifecycle defects break the promised bounded behavior and truthful presentation. All four changed documentation artifacts now reach both server.go and headwatch.go, and the cold harness installs the fresh workspace's observer. The aligned/staggered distinction and measured small-depth limits are correctly stated. No new authority-bearing decision chain is claimed for this assigned work; the separate policy proposal remains unadopted.\n\nSecurity: no keys, signatures, secret handling or grant of authority changes. A notification or old-frontier label still cannot authorize an act. Overlapping clock reads and accumulated pending probe requests leave resource-boundary defects. The earlier stale-UI-reply defect is fixed at this head. Preserve actual verified refusals for rewinds and missing refs and the existing current-verification boundary.\n\nSimplification: retain one clock mechanism and one bounded probe lifecycle. The per-probe in-flight flag and timer cleanup are not a bound on pending work across probes. Avoid parallel timer/request state that can outlive its wait; remove the test-process-only workaround if a simpler owned lifecycle makes it unnecessary. Keep the existing reporting vocabulary and explicit scope disposition.\n\nValidation: uncached service58.545s, MCP98.291s and docset68.863s tests pass; targeted vet passes. All211 UI tests and make ui-check pass, with tracked embedded output matching this head. All seventeen previews are ready and byte-exact; all four documentation artifacts reach both current clock and waiter artifacts. The candidates are live with disclosed ordinary inherited staleness and no describes_superseded_world flag when checked. The Go source is byte-identical to740672, whose four actual omissions (clock updates, shared wait reuse, last-release cancellation and retirement barrier) failed their intended tests. At4a661 the three-lifetime probe again fails in all three runs. Three actual UI omissions (late-answer guard, in-flight guard and release after failure) fail the shipped helper tests without syntax errors; the real hook passes both previous UI probes and fails the new cross-wait pending-request probe. The full optional performance lane was not rerun; its unchanged harness and retained measurements were inspected, without claiming depth20k or physical-resource evidence. Earlier review56ac47f5 was cancelled and its artifacts retired during filing, so no guarded verdict exists for740672; evidence0d686f2b remains in history. This verdict applies only to4a661.\n\nCorrect these on the existing implementation commitment, cite this finding in fresh exact-head artifacts, and request new independent review. Main's separate wireparity repair must land and complete CI before this outcome lands. Exact-path succession, source/workroom/receipt push, full CI and owned-worktree cleanup remain required.\n","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:87e16b2dbdd3c79d94fa2ff0698a07913934b79e","binding":"assigned","head":"4a661bc0a7e297433704360ad5fbf397bf7899c7","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:479584fd6e3eeed16a8f438df3e2e6ba0b8cd5e8\",\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:1005bb1e5965ec94d1fbc67ac53f6711d03243f3\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:2d521563cf60b09d287f04e71667ceae931d4356\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:1005bb1e5965ec94d1fbc67ac53f6711d03243f3","review_path":"reviewguard@1","stale":"true","stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:1005bb1e5965ec94d1fbc67ac53f6711d03243f3, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:159f58cc0e48fddd877bf885aabd048c1959d013, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:1e08f71bd8bb16a0db7e3f456f28207ab906a0bc, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:3e6fe9ce01988d25a41276969807e72d4efbb65c, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:40bcbe164d8783bd1e48fe1181138ab408297130, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:479584fd6e3eeed16a8f438df3e2e6ba0b8cd5e8, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:491607b5a3de26ea25b79e3dd8cb36cc8fc03218, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:66d1795ffd98044252e3c55cd8d6fa3b4fd1e55b, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:68900c0c40abfbe047b12a4cf71092748827c4a0, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:7b049e10127b330eef253385f363f44390029a69, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:87e16b2dbdd3c79d94fa2ff0698a07913934b79e, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:8df94cbdc933b22e4532dd3c1b8c093